#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-graphs — the box's two graphs in a browser, on loopback: token spend over time, and cc-vitals.

WHY. Two pictures existed only as still images and text. cc-vitals draws a PNG for the Slack tab and a block
sparkline for a terminal, and neither can be hovered, filtered or narrowed; the ledger had no time view at all,
only tables of totals (cc-spend --week is seven days summed, not seven days plotted). A browser gives both a
time axis you can interrogate, needs nothing installed, and is interactive for free.

ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH PER GRAPH, AND NEITHER IS HERE.
  /api/vitals   runs `cc-vitals series` and passes its JSON through untouched. Not a second reader of the vitals
                jsonl: the normalisation, the capacities, the colours and the one 0-100 axis (owner, 2026-08-30)
                are cc-vitals' and stay cc-vitals'. If it is wrong there it is wrong here, which is the point.
  /api/tokens   reads the ledger, ~/.cc/state/spend/<day>.tsv, and nothing else. cc-spend writes it; this only
                buckets it.

WHAT A POINT ON THE TOKEN GRAPH MEANS. The ledger is INCREMENTAL, not one row per day: cc-spend ticks every
TICK seconds and appends one row per (session, model) whose transcript grew, stamped at the LAST assistant
record in that chunk. So a row is spend that COMPLETED at its stamp, and a point is the spend the ledger booked
in that bucket. A chunk is at most one tick wide for a live session, which is why the default bucket is three
ticks: tick jitter cannot move spend across a bucket edge that matters.

WHY cc-econ's POPULATION TRAP DOES NOT APPLY HERE, and it is worth being explicit. That trap is about summing
two populations and about billing spend to a PR — the ledger sees every claude process, and a fix round RESUMES
the worker's session so its cost lands under the earlier iteration's id. This graph does neither. One population
(the ledger: every claude process with a transcript under ~/.claude/projects), and attribution is to a TIME and
to the row's own cwd/track/kind/model, all of which cc-spend recorded directly. A resumed session still stamps
its records at the second they happened, so the time axis is untouched by it; what a resumed session does move
is the SESSION id a line is grouped under, which is why `by=track` and `by=lane` are the defaults worth reading
and there is no by=session view.

THE ONE DERIVED FIELD IS `lane`, from cwd and kind, in this order:
  review   cwd under SCRATCH — the throwaway worktree a landing review runs in. cc-econ's own rule.
  worker   kind=headless and cwd under ~/.cc/worktrees — a track's loop iteration.
  session  kind=interactive — a person's or a planning session's transcript.
  other    everything else: probe and scratch dirs, a headless run outside a worktree, an unknown cwd.
Measured against 2026-09-02's 937 rows this placed 923 of them and all but $1 of the day.

WHAT IT EXCLUDES, said rather than drawn as zero.
  · Anything with no Claude Code transcript. The ledger's population is transcripts; an API call made outside a
    session is not in it and this cannot see it.
  · THE LEDGER'S DAY-RESOLUTION OPENING, at any bucket under a day. What is detected is a SIGNATURE, not a
    cause: a finished day holding exactly one row per (session, model) carries no shape within the day, so
    plotting it at 15-minute resolution would draw a spike that never happened. The usual cause is the ledger's
    first tick, which backfilled every day already on disk at one row per key.
    IT IS ONLY EVER A PREFIX. A backfill can only be the run of days that were on disk when the ledger started,
    so the scan stops at the first day that is not day-resolution. A quiet day LATER in the ledger — every
    session's transcript happening to grow inside one 5-minute tick — is a quiet day and is drawn, not blanked
    and then blamed on a backfill that did not touch it. Days in the prefix are dropped from sub-day series and
    reported in `coarse` with their totals, so the page can say so and offer the daily view, where they are
    exactly right.
  · USD is an estimate — cc-spend says so itself, tokens are the truth — so `usd` is a metric you can pick, not
    the default.
The last bucket is PARTIAL: it is not over, and the tick that will book its spend may not have run. It is
returned with partial=true and drawn as such, never as a cliff.

BINDING IS THE BOUNDARY. The server binds 127.0.0.1 and answers GET and HEAD only, from a fixed allowlist of
asset names — no directory listing, no traversal, no upload, no write path of any kind. Putting it on the
tailnet is one deliberate command and it is the owner's, not this script's:
    sudo tailscale serve --bg --https=443 http://127.0.0.1:<port>

  cc-graphs serve [--port N] [--foreground]   start (or restart) the server on 127.0.0.1
  cc-graphs stop                              stop it
  cc-graphs status                            running? on what port?
  cc-graphs tokens [--hours N] [--by lane|track|repo|model|kind] [--bucket SEC]   the series, JSON on stdout.
                    --bucket is one of 300 (a tick), 900, 3600 or 86400; without it the window picks one. A window
                    and bucket that would draw more than MAX_BUCKETS points is refused, not truncated.
  cc-graphs selfcheck                         tests; own fixtures — never this box's ledger and never its recorder
"""
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import urllib.parse
import zlib
from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone
from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer

H = os.path.expanduser("~")
LEDGER = os.environ.get("CC_SPEND_DIR") or os.path.join(H, ".cc", "state", "spend")
WORKTREES = os.environ.get("CC_WORKTREES") or os.path.join(H, ".cc", "worktrees")
STATE = os.environ.get("CC_GRAPHS_DIR") or os.path.join(H, ".cc", "state", "graphs")
BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
WEB = os.environ.get("CC_GRAPHS_WEB") or os.path.join(os.path.dirname(BIN), "web", "graphs")
VITALS = os.environ.get("CC_GRAPHS_VITALS") or os.path.join(BIN, "cc-vitals")

BIND = "127.0.0.1"            # NOT configurable, and that is the whole security model: the bind IS the boundary
PORT = int(os.environ.get("CC_GRAPHS_PORT") or 5190)   # 5180 is the lesson site's; this is the next free one
TICK = 300                    # cc-spend.timer's period: the widest a live session's ledger chunk can be
STEPS = (TICK, 900, 3600, 86400)                       # the bucket sizes offered, smallest = one tick
DAY = 86400
VITALS_TIMEOUT = 30           # a wedged renderer must never hold an HTTP thread
MAX_BUCKETS = 2000            # points on one chart before it is a request for a database, not a graph
MAX_GROUPS = 12               # lines on one chart before the tail folds into `other`; a 13th is unreadable anyway
SCRATCH = "/tmp/cc-" + "land" # the throwaway worktree a landing review or gate round runs in. The stem is bare
                              # on purpose: the lander names that directory <step>.<repo>.<pr>.<random> now and
                              # <random> before it, and this lane has to go on reading both
COLS = ["ts", "session", "cwd", "repo", "track", "kind", "model", "in", "out", "cache_read", "cache_write", "est_usd"]
NUM = ("in", "out", "cache_read", "cache_write", "usd")
BY = ("lane", "track", "repo", "model", "kind")
ASSETS = {"index.html": "text/html; charset=utf-8", "app.js": "text/javascript; charset=utf-8",
          "style.css": "text/css; charset=utf-8"}

# The same surface and the same categorical palette cc-vitals uses, in the reference's published order. Slots 1-6
# are the hues cc-vitals froze for its metrics; this is a different chart on a different page, so it starts at 1
# again and may use 7 and 8 too. Colour never identifies a line on its own here either: the legend is always on,
# it carries the number, and each line has its own dash.
SURFACE = {"mode": "dark", "bg": "#1a1a19", "ink": "#ffffff", "ink2": "#c3c2b7", "grid": "#383835"}
PALETTE = [("blue", "#3987e5", "solid"), ("orange", "#d95926", "6 2"), ("aqua", "#199e70", "2 2"),
           ("yellow", "#c98500", "8 3 2 3"), ("magenta", "#d55181", "1 3"), ("green", "#008300", "10 4"),
           ("violet", "#9085e9", "4 2 1 2"), ("red", "#e66767", "3 3")]
GREY = ("grey", "#c3c2b7", "1 2")            # `other` is never a hue: it is the leftovers, and it looks like it

LANES = {"review": "landing reviews", "worker": "track workers", "session": "sessions", "other": "other"}


def now():
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc)


def parse_ts(s):
    try:
        return datetime.strptime(s.strip(), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ").replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    except (ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None


def iso(epoch):
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(epoch))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the ledger (population: every claude process)

def ledger_days():
    """Every day the ledger has on disk, oldest first."""
    out = []
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(LEDGER, "*.tsv"))):
        d = os.path.basename(p)[:-4]
        if re.fullmatch(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}", d):
            out.append(d)
    return out


def read_day(day):
    """Every parsed row of one day file. A torn or hand-edited row is skipped, never a crash mid-report."""
    rows = []
    try:
        f = open(os.path.join(LEDGER, day + ".tsv"))
    except OSError:
        return rows
    with f:
        for line in f:
            if line.startswith("#"):
                continue
            p = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
            if len(p) < len(COLS):
                continue
            t = parse_ts(p[0])
            if t is None:
                continue
            r = {"t": t, "session": p[1], "cwd": p[2], "repo": p[3] or "-", "track": p[4] or "-",
                 "kind": p[5] or "-", "model": p[6] or "-"}
            try:
                r["in"], r["out"] = int(p[7]), int(p[8])
                r["cache_read"], r["cache_write"] = int(p[9]), int(p[10])
                r["usd"] = float(p[11])
            except ValueError:
                continue
            rows.append(r)
    return rows


def lane(row):
    """The one derived field — see the header. Review is tested FIRST: a review IS headless, in /tmp, and would
    otherwise fall through to `other`."""
    cwd = row.get("cwd") or ""
    if cwd.startswith(SCRATCH):
        return "review"
    if row.get("kind") == "headless" and cwd.startswith(WORKTREES + os.sep):
        return "worker"
    if row.get("kind") == "interactive":
        return "session"
    return "other"


def coarse_day(day, rows, upto):
    """Is this day DAY-RESOLUTION — one ledger row per (session, model), so no shape within the day?

    The SIGNATURE only. `rows == distinct keys` is what a backfilled day looks like, and it is also what a quiet
    day looks like when every session's transcript grew inside one tick — the two are indistinguishable from one
    day's file, which is why the caller (coarse_prefix) decides WHICH days it may be believed about.

    A day still in progress is NEVER day-resolution: this morning's file legitimately has one row per session so
    far, and blanking it would hide today."""
    if not rows:
        return False
    if day >= upto.strftime("%Y-%m-%d"):
        return False
    keys = {(r["session"], r["model"]) for r in rows}
    return len(rows) == len(keys)


def coarse_prefix(days, upto):
    """The day-resolution OPENING of the ledger: days[0] and every day after it that is still day-resolution,
    stopping at the first that is not.

    The finding this exists for: `rows == distinct keys` alone also matches a genuinely quiet day, and blanking
    such a day dropped real spend from every sub-day window while the page told the reader a backfill caused it.
    A backfill cannot do that — it ran once, over the days already on disk, so it can only ever produce an
    unbroken run from the ledger's first day. After the first normal day, a day-resolution day is a quiet day and
    is drawn like one."""
    out = set()
    for day in days:                      # cheap: a day-resolution day is one row per (session, model)
        if not coarse_day(day, read_day(day), upto):
            break
        out.add(day)
    return out


def auto_step(hours):
    """The bucket a window gets when none is asked for. Wider windows get wider buckets so the line stays
    readable; > 7 days is daily, which is also the resolution the backfilled days really have."""
    if hours <= 6:
        return TICK
    if hours <= 48:
        return 900
    if hours <= 24 * 7:
        return 3600
    return DAY


def group_of(row, by):
    if by == "lane":
        return row["_lane"]
    return row.get(by) or "-"


def label_of(key, by):
    if by == "lane":
        return LANES.get(key, key)
    return "(none)" if key == "-" else key      # the ledger writes "-" for a column it had no value for


def tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=None, upto=None):
    """The token series: one bucket per `step` seconds over the last `hours`, grouped by `by`.

    A point is the spend the ledger BOOKED in that bucket — see the header for what that means and what it
    leaves out. Nothing here attributes spend to a PR, so none of cc-econ's attribution rules are in play."""
    if by not in BY:
        raise ValueError("by must be one of " + ", ".join(BY))
    hours = max(1, min(int(hours), 24 * 90))
    step = int(step) if step else auto_step(hours)
    if step not in STEPS:
        raise ValueError("bucket must be one of " + ", ".join(str(s) for s in STEPS))
    upto = upto or now()
    end = int(upto.timestamp())
    start = end - hours * 3600
    t0 = (start // step) * step                       # buckets are aligned to the epoch, so a daily one is UTC midnight
    n = (end - t0) // step + 1
    if n > MAX_BUCKETS:                               # 90 days at one tick is 26 000 points: a request, not a chart
        raise ValueError("%d buckets: widen --bucket or shorten --hours (max %d)" % (n, MAX_BUCKETS))
    sub_day = step < DAY

    coarse, kept = [], []
    days = ledger_days()
    prefix = coarse_prefix(days, upto) if sub_day else set()   # only ever a PREFIX — see coarse_prefix
    # Before the ledger's first day there is no answer, and no answer is not zero. A flat zero line running back
    # to the start of a 30-day window says the box spent nothing that fortnight; it says nothing of the kind.
    # A day AFTER that first one with no file IS zero: cc-spend writes a file whenever a transcript grew.
    start_known = (datetime.strptime(days[0], "%Y-%m-%d").replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp()) if days else end
    for day in days:
        # A day file is read whole — coarseness is a property of the whole day, not of the window's slice of it.
        d0 = datetime.strptime(day, "%Y-%m-%d").replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
        if d0.timestamp() > end or (d0 + timedelta(days=1)).timestamp() < t0:
            continue
        rows = read_day(day)
        inwin = [r for r in rows if t0 <= r["t"].timestamp() <= end]
        if not inwin:
            continue
        if day in prefix:
            tot = {k: sum(r[k] for r in inwin) for k in NUM}
            tot["rows"] = len(inwin)
            tot["day"] = day
            tot["from"] = max(t0, int(d0.timestamp()))
            tot["to"] = min(end, int((d0 + timedelta(days=1)).timestamp()) - 1)
            coarse.append(tot)
            continue
        kept.extend(inwin)

    # Every step divides 86400 and t0 is epoch-aligned to step, so no bucket straddles UTC midnight: blinding a
    # coarse day's buckets can never blank a bucket that also holds a resolvable day's rows.
    # A day this window cannot resolve is drawn as NOTHING, never as zero. Six backfilled days behind one live one
    # read as "the box spent nothing all week" if their buckets hold 0, and the banner saying otherwise loses to
    # the flat line every time. A null breaks the line, which is what no-data-at-this-resolution looks like.
    blind = set()
    for i in range(n):                                # whole buckets that end before the ledger begins
        if t0 + (i + 1) * step <= start_known:
            blind.add(i)
    for c in coarse:
        for i in range((c["from"] - t0) // step, (c["to"] - t0) // step + 1):
            if 0 <= i < n:
                blind.add(i)

    for r in kept:
        r["_lane"] = lane(r)

    # Ranked by USD even though tokens is the default metric: a token is not comparable across models (a cheap
    # model's million is a dear one's thousand), and the fold is asking which lines MATTER. The estimate is the
    # only cross-model measure the ledger has. It fixes which groups survive, never their colour or their values.
    order = {}
    for r in kept:
        order[group_of(r, by)] = order.get(group_of(r, by), 0.0) + r["usd"]
    top = [k for k, _ in sorted(order.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1])[:MAX_GROUPS]]
    fold = len(order) > len(top)
    keep = set(top)

    series = {}
    for k in top + (["other"] if fold and "other" not in top else []):
        series[k] = {k2: [0] * n for k2 in NUM}
    for r in kept:
        g = group_of(r, by)
        if g not in keep:
            g = "other"
        i = int(r["t"].timestamp() - t0) // step
        if not 0 <= i < n:
            continue
        for k2 in NUM:
            series[g][k2][i] += r[k2]

    for g in series.values():
        for arr in g.values():
            for i in blind:
                arr[i] = None

    def ssum(a):
        return sum(v for v in a if v is not None)

    # A slot is a function of the group's NAME alone — crc32 picks it — because the page refreshes every minute
    # and a line that changes colour under the reader is worse than one whose colour they check against the
    # legend anyway. Neither the rank nor WHICH groups survived the fold may move it: an index over the surviving
    # names (what this was) re-coloured every line below a newcomer the moment a 13th track outspent the 12th.
    # The one thing that still moves a line is a genuine hash COLLISION: two names wanting the same slot, settled
    # by walking to the next free one in sorted order, so the later name yields. With more groups than colours
    # the palette fills and the rest take their crc32 slot as-is, sharing a hue — the legend and the per-slot
    # dash are what tell those apart, here as everywhere else on this chart.
    slot, taken = {}, set()
    for k in sorted(k for k in series if k != "other"):
        i = zlib.crc32(k.encode()) % len(PALETTE)
        while i in taken and len(taken) < len(PALETTE):
            i = (i + 1) % len(PALETTE)
        taken.add(i)
        slot[k] = i
    groups = []
    for k in sorted(series, key=lambda k: -order.get(k, 0.0)):
        hue, colour, dash = GREY if k == "other" else PALETTE[slot[k] % len(PALETTE)]
        g = {"key": k, "label": label_of(k, by), "hue": hue, "colour": colour, "dash": dash}
        g.update({k2: series[k][k2] for k2 in NUM})
        g["tokens"] = [None if j in blind else sum(series[k][c][j] for c in ("in", "out", "cache_read", "cache_write"))
                       for j in range(n)]
        g["total"] = {k2: ssum(series[k][k2]) for k2 in NUM}
        g["total"]["tokens"] = ssum(g["tokens"])
        groups.append(g)

    return {
        "generated": iso(time.time()), "window_h": hours, "by": by, "t0": t0, "step": step, "n": n,
        "partial": True,                              # the last bucket is not over and its tick may not have run
        "surface": SURFACE, "groups": groups,
        "total": {k2: sum(g["total"][k2] for g in groups) for k2 in list(NUM) + ["tokens"]},
        "rows": len(kept), "folded": fold, "max_groups": MAX_GROUPS, "tick": TICK,
        "coarse": {"days": coarse, "usd": sum(c["usd"] for c in coarse), "blind": len(blind),
                   "ledger_from": days[0] if days else None},
        "means": ("spend the ledger booked in the bucket: cc-spend stamps each chunk at the last assistant "
                  "record in it, so a point is spend that COMPLETED then"),
        "excludes": ("anything with no Claude Code transcript; and, below daily buckets, the ledger's opening "
                     "days that hold one row per session-model and so carry no shape within the day (usually "
                     "the first tick, which backfilled what was already on disk). Their buckets are null, not "
                     "zero: the line is BLANK over them, because no-data-at-this-resolution must not look like "
                     "a quiet week"),
        "lanes": LANES,
    }


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- vitals (passthrough; cc-vitals owns the truth)

def vitals(hours=24):
    hours = max(1, min(int(hours), 24 * 3))
    p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, VITALS, "series", "--hours", str(hours)],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=VITALS_TIMEOUT)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError((p.stderr or "cc-vitals series failed").strip()[:400])
    return json.loads(p.stdout)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the server (GET/HEAD, loopback, allowlist)

class Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    server_version = "cc-graphs"
    sys_version = ""

    def log_request(self, code="-", size="-"):
        if isinstance(code, int) and 200 <= code < 400:   # a refresh a minute must not grow serve.log forever
            return
        self.log_message('"%s" %s', self.requestline, code)

    def log_message(self, fmt, *a):
        sys.stderr.write("%s %s\n" % (time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()), fmt % a))

    def _send(self, code, body, ctype, head=False):
        body = body if isinstance(body, bytes) else body.encode()
        self.send_response(code)
        self.send_header("Content-Type", ctype)
        self.send_header("Content-Length", str(len(body)))
        self.send_header("Cache-Control", "no-store")
        self.end_headers()
        if not head:
            self.wfile.write(body)

    def _json(self, code, obj, head=False):
        self._send(code, json.dumps(obj), "application/json; charset=utf-8", head)

    def do_HEAD(self):
        self.route(head=True)

    def do_GET(self):
        self.route(head=False)

    def route(self, head=False):
        u = urllib.parse.urlparse(self.path)
        q = urllib.parse.parse_qs(u.query)
        one = lambda k, d=None: (q.get(k) or [d])[0]
        path = u.path
        try:
            if path == "/api/tokens":
                return self._json(200, tokens(hours=int(one("hours", 24)), by=one("by", "lane"),
                                              step=one("bucket")), head)
            if path == "/api/vitals":
                return self._json(200, vitals(hours=int(one("hours", 24))), head)
            if path.startswith("/api/"):
                return self._json(404, {"error": "no such endpoint"}, head)
        except ValueError as e:
            return self._json(400, {"error": str(e)}, head)
        except Exception as e:                        # a broken input must not take the server down
            return self._json(500, {"error": "%s: %s" % (type(e).__name__, e)}, head)

        name = "index.html" if path == "/" else path.lstrip("/")
        if name not in ASSETS:                        # an exact allowlist: no listing, no traversal, no guessing
            return self._send(404, "not found\n", "text/plain; charset=utf-8", head)
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(WEB, name), "rb") as f:
                return self._send(200, f.read(), ASSETS[name], head)
        except OSError:
            return self._send(500, "asset missing: %s\n" % name, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", head)

    def do_POST(self):                                # there is no write path here, and never should be
        self._send(405, "read-only\n", "text/plain; charset=utf-8")

    do_PUT = do_DELETE = do_PATCH = do_POST


def pidfile():
    return os.path.join(STATE, "serve.pid")


def portfile():
    return os.path.join(STATE, "serve.port")


def running():
    """The pid of OUR server, or None. The cmdline check is what stops a recycled pid reading as running."""
    try:
        with open(pidfile()) as f:
            pid = int(f.read().strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    try:
        with open("/proc/%d/cmdline" % pid, "rb") as f:
            if b"cc-graphs" not in f.read():
                return None
    except OSError:
        return None
    return pid


def stop(_opt=None, quiet=False):
    pid = running()
    if not pid:
        if not quiet:
            print("cc-graphs: not running")
    else:
        os.kill(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
        for _ in range(40):
            if not running():
                break
            time.sleep(0.1)
        if running():
            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        if not quiet:
            print("cc-graphs: stopped (pid %d)" % pid)
    for p in (pidfile(), portfile()):
        try:
            os.remove(p)
        except OSError:
            pass
    return 0


def status(_opt=None):
    pid = running()
    if not pid:
        print("cc-graphs: not running")
        return 1
    try:
        with open(portfile()) as f:
            port = f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        port = "?"
    print("cc-graphs: running (pid %d) on http://%s:%s/" % (pid, BIND, port))
    return 0


def run(port):
    os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
    srv = ThreadingHTTPServer((BIND, port), Handler)
    with open(pidfile(), "w") as f:
        f.write(str(os.getpid()))
    with open(portfile(), "w") as f:
        f.write(str(port))
    sys.stderr.write("cc-graphs: serving http://%s:%d/ (assets %s)\n" % (BIND, port, WEB))
    try:
        srv.serve_forever()
    finally:
        srv.server_close()


def serve(opt):
    port = int(opt.get("--port") or PORT)
    stop(quiet=True)                                  # serve is a restart; "not running" is not news to whoever ran it
    os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
    if "--foreground" in opt:
        return run(port)
    log = os.path.join(STATE, "serve.log")
    with open(log, "ab") as out:
        subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "serve", "--port", str(port), "--foreground"],
                         stdout=out, stderr=out, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True)
    for _ in range(50):
        time.sleep(0.1)
        if running():
            break
    if not running():
        print("cc-graphs: failed to start — see %s" % log, file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print("cc-graphs: http://%s:%d/   (loopback only — putting it on the tailnet is yours:\n"
          "           sudo tailscale serve --bg --https=443 http://%s:%d)" % (BIND, port, BIND, port))
    return 0


def cmd_tokens(opt):
    print(json.dumps(tokens(hours=int(opt.get("--hours") or 24), by=opt.get("--by") or "lane",
                            step=opt.get("--bucket")), indent=1))
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- selfcheck (own fixtures; no ledger, no network)

def _fixture(tmp, day, rows):
    """Write one fixture ledger day. Every case builds the state it asserts on; none reads another's."""
    os.makedirs(tmp, exist_ok=True)
    with open(os.path.join(tmp, day + ".tsv"), "w") as f:
        f.write("#" + "\t".join(COLS) + "\n")
        for r in rows:
            f.write("\t".join(str(x) for x in r) + "\n")


def _row(ts, sess="s1", cwd="/w", repo="r", track="t", kind="headless", model="m",
         i=1, o=2, cr=3, cw=4, usd=1.0):
    return [ts, sess, cwd, repo, track, kind, model, i, o, cr, cw, "%.6f" % usd]


def selfcheck(_opt=None):
    global LEDGER, WORKTREES, WEB, VITALS
    ok, bad = 0, []

    def t(name, cond):
        nonlocal ok
        if cond:
            ok += 1
        else:
            bad.append(name)

    keep_l, keep_w, keep_web, keep_v = LEDGER, WORKTREES, WEB, VITALS
    root = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-graphs-selfcheck-")
    try:
        WORKTREES = os.path.join(root, "wt")
        upto = datetime(2026, 5, 4, 12, 0, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc)
        today, past = "2026-05-04", "2026-05-03"

        # 1 — lane, all four, and the one that has to be tested first: a review IS headless and IS in /tmp, so
        #     `review` before `worker`/`other` is the whole rule. Both the kept and the suppressed answer asserted.
        #     The two cwds are written out rather than built from SCRATCH: a fixture made of the constant it is
        #     testing passes whatever that constant says, which is how the older spelling went unnoticed here.
        NEW, OLD = "/tmp/cc-land.review.myrepo.7.ab12/head", "/tmp/cc-land-ab12/x"
        t("lane review", lane({"cwd": NEW, "kind": "headless"}) == "review")
        t("lane review not worker", lane({"cwd": NEW, "kind": "headless"}) != "worker")
        # …and the bare name the lander made before it put the repo and the step in the directory: the ledger still
        # holds those rows, and reading only the new spelling empties this line into `other`.
        t("lane review, the older name too", lane({"cwd": OLD, "kind": "headless"}) == "review")
        t("lane worker", lane({"cwd": WORKTREES + "/repo/track", "kind": "headless"}) == "worker")
        t("lane session", lane({"cwd": os.path.join(root, "dev"), "kind": "interactive"}) == "session")
        t("lane other", lane({"cwd": "/tmp/probe-x", "kind": "headless"}) == "other")
        t("lane worker needs headless", lane({"cwd": WORKTREES + "/r/t", "kind": "interactive"}) == "session")
        # a cwd that merely STARTS with the worktrees string is not inside it
        t("lane worktree prefix is not a parent", lane({"cwd": WORKTREES + "-else/x", "kind": "headless"}) == "other")

        # 2 — a row lands in the bucket holding its stamp, and two rows a bucket apart do not merge.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l2")
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:10Z", usd=2.0), _row("2026-05-04T11:02:00Z", usd=3.0),
                                 _row("2026-05-04T11:20:00Z", usd=5.0)])
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=900, upto=upto)
        g = r["groups"][0]
        i1 = int(datetime(2026, 5, 4, 11, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp() - r["t0"]) // 900
        i2 = int(datetime(2026, 5, 4, 11, 15, tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp() - r["t0"]) // 900
        t("bucket sums same window", abs(g["usd"][i1] - 5.0) < 1e-9)
        t("bucket keeps later row apart", abs(g["usd"][i2] - 5.0) < 1e-9 and i1 != i2)
        t("bucket total", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - 10.0) < 1e-9)
        t("tokens is the four counts", g["tokens"][i1] == 2 * (1 + 2 + 3 + 4))
        t("last bucket flagged partial", r["partial"] is True)
        t("buckets are epoch-aligned", r["t0"] % 900 == 0)

        # 3 — the window is a filter: a row before t0 is gone, one inside is kept.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l3")
        _fixture(LEDGER, past, [_row("2026-05-03T01:00:00Z", usd=9.0)])
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="a", usd=1.0),
                                 _row("2026-05-04T11:05:00Z", sess="b", usd=1.0)])
        r = tokens(hours=6, by="lane", step=900, upto=upto)
        t("window drops the older row", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - 2.0) < 1e-9)
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=DAY, upto=upto)
        t("wider window keeps it", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - 11.0) < 1e-9)

        # 4 — a backfilled day (one row per (session, model), and the day is over) is DROPPED below daily buckets
        #     and INCLUDED at daily ones. Both halves asserted, on state this case built.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l4")
        _fixture(LEDGER, past, [_row("2026-05-03T23:00:00Z", sess="a", usd=4.0),
                                _row("2026-05-03T23:00:00Z", sess="b", usd=6.0)])
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="c", usd=1.0),
                                 _row("2026-05-04T11:30:00Z", sess="c", usd=1.0)])
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=900, upto=upto)
        t("coarse day not drawn", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - 2.0) < 1e-9)
        t("coarse day reported", [c["day"] for c in r["coarse"]["days"]] == [past]
          and abs(r["coarse"]["usd"] - 10.0) < 1e-9)
        # the half that matters: blank over the coarse day, a real 0 where the box simply spent nothing
        blind_i = int(datetime(2026, 5, 3, 23, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp() - r["t0"]) // 900
        seen_i = int(datetime(2026, 5, 4, 11, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp() - r["t0"]) // 900
        quiet_i = int(datetime(2026, 5, 4, 8, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp() - r["t0"]) // 900
        g0 = r["groups"][0]
        t("coarse buckets are null, not zero", g0["usd"][blind_i] is None and g0["tokens"][blind_i] is None)
        t("a drawn bucket is a number", g0["usd"][seen_i] == 1.0)
        t("a genuinely quiet bucket is zero, not null", g0["usd"][quiet_i] == 0)
        t("nulls are left out of the total", abs(g0["total"]["usd"] - 2.0) < 1e-9)
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=DAY, upto=upto)
        t("coarse day drawn at daily", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - 12.0) < 1e-9 and not r["coarse"]["days"])

        # 4b — before the ledger's first day the answer is BLANK; after it, an empty bucket is a real zero.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l4b")
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="a", usd=1.0),
                                 _row("2026-05-04T11:30:00Z", sess="a", usd=1.0)])
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=900, upto=upto)
        g0 = r["groups"][0]
        before = int(datetime(2026, 5, 3, 20, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp() - r["t0"]) // 900
        after = int(datetime(2026, 5, 4, 5, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc).timestamp() - r["t0"]) // 900
        t("before the ledger starts is blank", g0["usd"][before] is None)
        t("a quiet bucket after it is zero", g0["usd"][after] == 0)
        t("the ledger's first day is reported", r["coarse"]["ledger_from"] == today)

        # 4d — THE PREFIX RULE (review finding, PR #199). `rows == distinct keys` also matches a genuinely quiet
        #      day, so it may only be believed about the ledger's OPENING run. Both halves on one fixture: the
        #      opening day is blanked and reported, and a quiet day with the SAME signature AFTER a normal day is
        #      drawn — which is the spend the old rule dropped while telling the reader a backfill caused it.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l4d")
        _fixture(LEDGER, "2026-05-01", [_row("2026-05-01T13:00:00Z", sess=x, usd=4.0) for x in ("a", "b")])
        _fixture(LEDGER, "2026-05-02", [_row("2026-05-02T13:00:00Z", sess="c", usd=1.0),
                                        _row("2026-05-02T13:30:00Z", sess="c", usd=1.0)])
        _fixture(LEDGER, "2026-05-03", [_row("2026-05-03T13:00:00Z", sess=x, usd=3.0) for x in ("d", "e")])
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="f", usd=5.0)])
        r = tokens(hours=72, by="lane", step=900, upto=upto)
        g0 = r["groups"][0]
        at = lambda dt_: int(dt_.timestamp() - r["t0"]) // 900
        near = lambda v, x: v is not None and abs(v - x) < 1e-9      # a blanked bucket must FAIL, not raise
        opening = at(datetime(2026, 5, 1, 13, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc))
        normal = at(datetime(2026, 5, 2, 13, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc))
        quiet = at(datetime(2026, 5, 3, 13, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc))
        t("the opening day is still blanked", g0["usd"][opening] is None)
        t("only the opening day is reported coarse", [c["day"] for c in r["coarse"]["days"]] == ["2026-05-01"]
          and abs(r["coarse"]["usd"] - 8.0) < 1e-9)
        t("the day that breaks the run is drawn", near(g0["usd"][normal], 1.0))
        t("a quiet day AFTER it is drawn, not blamed on a backfill", near(g0["usd"][quiet], 6.0))
        t("its spend is in the total", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - 13.0) < 1e-9)

        # 4c — an empty ledger answers, it does not crash: no lines, no totals, nothing claimed.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "empty")
        os.makedirs(LEDGER)
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=900, upto=upto)
        t("empty ledger answers", r["groups"] == [] and r["total"]["usd"] == 0 and r["rows"] == 0)
        t("empty ledger claims no first day", r["coarse"]["ledger_from"] is None)

        # 5 — today is never coarse: this morning legitimately has one row per session so far.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l5")
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T09:00:00Z", sess="a", usd=1.0),
                                 _row("2026-05-04T10:00:00Z", sess="b", usd=1.0)])
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=900, upto=upto)
        t("day in progress is drawn", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - 2.0) < 1e-9)
        t("day in progress is not coarse", not r["coarse"]["days"])

        # 6 — more groups than fit fold into `other`, and folding loses nothing.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l6")
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="s%d" % k, track="t%02d" % k, usd=float(k + 1))
                                 for k in range(MAX_GROUPS + 5)])
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="track", step=900, upto=upto)
        want = sum(float(k + 1) for k in range(MAX_GROUPS + 5))
        t("fold keeps every dollar", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - want) < 1e-6)
        t("fold caps the lines", len(r["groups"]) <= MAX_GROUPS + 1 and r["folded"])
        t("fold has an other line", any(g["key"] == "other" for g in r["groups"]))
        t("biggest track survives folding", any(g["key"] == "t%02d" % (MAX_GROUPS + 4) for g in r["groups"]))
        # the colour a group gets must not move when the ranking does. Same five groups both times — a set that
        # folds would change WHICH groups survive, which is a different question from this one.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l6b")
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="s%d" % k, track="t%d" % k, usd=float(k + 1))
                                 for k in range(5)])
        up = tokens(hours=24, by="track", step=900, upto=upto)
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="s%d" % k, track="t%d" % k, usd=float(5 - k))
                                 for k in range(5)])
        down = tokens(hours=24, by="track", step=900, upto=upto)
        c1 = {g["key"]: g["colour"] for g in up["groups"]}
        t("the ranking really did flip",
          [g["key"] for g in down["groups"]] == list(reversed([g["key"] for g in up["groups"]])))
        t("colour follows the name, not the rank", all(c1[g["key"]] == g["colour"] for g in down["groups"]))
        # 6c — and not the MEMBERSHIP either (review finding, PR #199): an index over the surviving names moved
        #      every line below a newcomer. These five names hash to five different slots, so what this case
        #      isolates is membership, not a collision — a collision is settled by walking to the next free slot
        #      and CAN move the later name, which the comment beside the code says outright.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l6c")
        four = [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="s%d" % k, track="t%d" % k, usd=float(k)) for k in (1, 2, 3, 4)]
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, four)
        before = tokens(hours=24, by="track", step=900, upto=upto)
        # the newcomer sorts FIRST and outspends them all, which is the shape that shifted an index-based slot
        _fixture(LEDGER, today, four + [_row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", sess="s0", track="t0", usd=99.0)])
        after = tokens(hours=24, by="track", step=900, upto=upto)
        cb = {g["key"]: g["colour"] for g in before["groups"]}
        ca = {g["key"]: g["colour"] for g in after["groups"]}
        t("the membership really did change", set(ca) - set(cb) == {"t0"} and after["groups"][0]["key"] == "t0")
        t("a newcomer re-colours nobody", all(ca[k] == cb[k] for k in cb))
        t("and the survivors still differ from each other", len(set(ca.values())) == len(ca))

        # 7 — a torn row is skipped, not a crash, and the good row beside it still counts.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l7")
        os.makedirs(LEDGER)
        with open(os.path.join(LEDGER, today + ".tsv"), "w") as f:
            f.write("#" + "\t".join(COLS) + "\n")
            f.write("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z\tbroken\n")
            f.write("\t".join(str(x) for x in _row("2026-05-04T11:00:00Z", usd=7.0)) + "\n")
            f.write("not-a-stamp\ts\t/w\tr\tt\theadless\tm\t1\t1\t1\t1\t1.0\n")
        r = tokens(hours=24, by="lane", step=900, upto=upto)
        t("torn rows skipped, good row kept", abs(r["total"]["usd"] - 7.0) < 1e-9 and r["rows"] == 1)

        # 8 — the bucket a window gets on its own, at both sides of each edge. On an empty ledger of its own:
        #     these are the window arithmetic and must not read the rows case 7 happened to leave behind.
        LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l8")
        os.makedirs(LEDGER)
        t("auto 6h", auto_step(6) == TICK and auto_step(7) == 900)
        t("auto 48h", auto_step(48) == 900 and auto_step(49) == 3600)
        t("auto 7d", auto_step(24 * 7) == 3600 and auto_step(24 * 7 + 1) == DAY)
        t("bad bucket refused", _raises(lambda: tokens(hours=24, step=77, upto=upto)))
        t("too many buckets refused", _raises(lambda: tokens(hours=24 * 90, step=TICK, upto=upto)))
        t("that same window is fine daily", tokens(hours=24 * 90, step=DAY, upto=upto)["n"] <= MAX_BUCKETS)
        t("bad group refused", _raises(lambda: tokens(hours=24, by="session", upto=upto)))

        # 9 — the server: loopback, read-only, exact asset allowlist.
        WEB = os.path.join(root, "web")
        os.makedirs(WEB)
        for a in ASSETS:
            with open(os.path.join(WEB, a), "w") as f:
                f.write("/*%s*/" % a)
        with open(os.path.join(root, "secret"), "w") as f:
            f.write("no")
        t("bind is loopback", BIND == "127.0.0.1")
        import threading
        import urllib.request
        quiet = Handler.log_message
        Handler.log_message = lambda *a, **k: None      # the cases are the output; an access log is not
        srv = ThreadingHTTPServer((BIND, 0), Handler)
        threading.Thread(target=srv.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()
        base = "http://%s:%d" % (BIND, srv.server_port)

        def get(p, method="GET"):
            rq = urllib.request.Request(base + p, method=method)
            try:
                with urllib.request.urlopen(rq, timeout=5) as resp:
                    return resp.status, resp.read()
            except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
                return e.code, e.read()
        try:
            t("index served", get("/")[0] == 200 and get("/")[1].startswith(b"/*index.html"))
            t("asset served", get("/app.js")[0] == 200)
            t("head has no body", get("/app.js", "HEAD")[1] == b"")
            t("unknown asset refused", get("/secret")[0] == 404)
            t("traversal refused", get("/../secret")[0] == 404 and get("/%2e%2e/secret")[0] == 404)
            t("post refused", get("/", "POST")[0] == 405)
            t("unknown api is 404 json", get("/api/nope")[0] == 404)
            t("bad query is 400 not 500", get("/api/tokens?by=nope")[0] == 400)
            # A fixture of its own, and stamped NOW: the endpoint asks the real clock, not this case's `upto`.
            # The earlier day of zero-cost rows is there so that "before the ledger begins is blank" cannot blank
            # the row itself when this runs in the first minute after UTC midnight — the total is 3.5 either way.
            LEDGER = os.path.join(root, "l9")
            yday = (now() - timedelta(days=1)).strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
            _fixture(LEDGER, yday, [_row(yday + "T00:00:0%dZ" % k, i=0, o=0, cr=0, cw=0, usd=0.0) for k in (0, 1)])
            _fixture(LEDGER, now().strftime("%Y-%m-%d"), [_row(iso(time.time() - 60), usd=3.5)])
            code, body = get("/api/tokens?hours=24&bucket=900")
            j = json.loads(body)
            t("tokens endpoint is the same function, on this case's own ledger",
              code == 200 and j["step"] == 900 and abs(j["total"]["usd"] - 3.5) < 1e-9)

            # 10 — /api/vitals is a PASSTHROUGH and nothing else: whatever cc-vitals prints comes back unchanged.
            #      On a stub written here, because this case must never run this box's own recorder. Both sides:
            #      the object that is passed through, and the cc-vitals that fails — which is an error the page
            #      can show, not a server that fell over with it.
            VITALS = os.path.join(root, "stub-vitals.py")
            spec = {"generated": "2026-05-04T12:00:00Z", "n": 2, "step": 60, "t0": 1,
                    "series": [{"key": "cpu", "norm": [1.0, None]}]}
            with open(VITALS, "w") as f:
                f.write("import sys\nsys.stdout.write(%r)\n" % json.dumps(spec))
            t("vitals passes cc-vitals through unchanged", vitals(hours=6) == spec)
            code, body = get("/api/vitals?hours=6")
            t("vitals endpoint serves that same object", code == 200 and json.loads(body) == spec)
            with open(VITALS, "w") as f:
                f.write("import sys\nsys.stderr.write('recorder is off\\n')\nsys.exit(1)\n")
            code, body = get("/api/vitals?hours=6")
            t("a failing cc-vitals is an error the page can show",
              code == 500 and "recorder is off" in json.loads(body)["error"])
            t("and the server is still up after it", get("/")[0] == 200)
        finally:
            srv.shutdown()
            srv.server_close()
            Handler.log_message = quiet
    finally:
        LEDGER, WORKTREES, WEB, VITALS = keep_l, keep_w, keep_web, keep_v
        shutil.rmtree(root, ignore_errors=True)

    print("cc-graphs selfcheck: %d passed, %d failed" % (ok, len(bad)))
    for b in bad:
        print("  FAIL", b)
    return 1 if bad else 0


def _raises(fn):
    try:
        fn()
    except ValueError:
        return True
    return False


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cli

def flags(argv):
    opt = {}
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a.startswith("--") and i + 1 < len(argv) and not argv[i + 1].startswith("--"):
            opt[a] = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
        else:
            opt[a] = True
            i += 1
    return opt


def main(argv):
    cmds = {"serve": serve, "stop": stop, "status": status, "tokens": cmd_tokens, "selfcheck": selfcheck}
    cmd = argv[0] if argv else ""
    if cmd not in cmds or "--help" in argv or "-h" in argv:
        print(__doc__.strip(), file=sys.stderr if cmd not in cmds else sys.stdout)
        return 2 if cmd not in cmds else 0
    return cmds[cmd](flags(argv[1:]))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
